# Names the doctrine may cite that the tree does not define, each with
# its reason. A new entry here is a DECISION, not a convenience.
emit_loop: a NEGATIVE example — the law names it as a shape it refuses (a feature-named fn on a pass context)
get_owned: prose shortening of `avra_array_get_owned`, in a sentence that spells the full name beside it
fn_params: a NEGATIVE example — the entry names four sibling projections that WERE written and were replaced by `fn_parts`
filter_map: a construct on the sugar backlog, named as absent — the entry licenses a repetition until it lands
aligned_marks: a NEGATIVE example — the law names the window helper whose anchor list held one KIND of member and silently attached a mark to the wrong one; the anchor list that replaced it is `MarkWindows`
innermost_holding: a HISTORICAL wanting site — the sugar backlog names it as a scanning fn that motivated the `continue` ask; the trivia cursor deleted it when it replaced the scans
first_start_after: a HISTORICAL wanting site — the same ask's other site, deleted by the trivia cursor with its sibling
file_command: a HISTORICAL example — the "one definition" instance the law named for the CLI's shared registry; @std/cli as components replaced that registry
unified_lift: a HISTORICAL instance — I31 names it as a fn that wore `accepts`'s doc; the generic seat law folded it into `agreed`, which judges a concrete seat by `accepts` alone
under_overlay: a HISTORICAL instance — I15 names it as the bracket fn taking a thunk that its matcher once found; the brackets are spelled at their sites now (lane C, S4b-2) and I15 is a native rule whose fixtures carry the shape
